Freedom: refuse private destinations for connections from user inbounds

FORK DEVIATION: not upstream code. Upstream blocks private destinations of user-inbound connections in two
steps the pin does not have: v26.4.15 (310b764811aa) added ipsBlocked with a default that closes the stream,
and v26.5.3 (4e87f59628ff) replaced it with finalRules and a default rule that blackholes the stream unless a
finalRules allow matches; both build on common/geodata from the geodata refactor 82624bc. This patch keeps what
the node needs on v26.3.27: a connection whose inbound is vless, vmess, trojan, hysteria, wireguard or any
shadowsocks inbound, the inbounds xray-freedom-local-host.patch guards, may not reach an address of the
geoip:private list of the geoip.dat the image ships, written here as 18 explicit prefixes so no asset is read,
unless the freedom outbound's finalRules (upstream's JSON shape: action allow or block, network, port and ip, the
first match decides) allow it, or a privateAllow entry (ruleTag and ip) covers it and the router's first match for
the connection with that address as its target is the routing rule with that ruleTag, sending to this same
outbound. The router check runs app/router's rules without their webhooks or balancers (MatchingRule), so an
earlier rule that blocks the address, or a rule for another inbound, user, source or outbound, keeps the address
refused. The local host stays refused whatever an allow says. finalRules decide private destinations only, so public
destinations and the dokodemo-door, socks and http inbounds behave as before, and a stock v26.3.27 loads the same
config without guarding it.

Where freedom checks: a literal or freedom-resolved address before the dial; a name freedom resolves itself
(domainStrategy) takes its random answer among those it would dial, so a private answer next to a public one is
never picked, for the connection and for each datagram alike. When the outbound reaches the default
system dialer directly (no proxySettings, no dialerProxy, the raw TCP transport for TCP, no alternative system
dialer: app/proxyman/outbound's AppliesConnectCheck), freedom does no lookup of its own and puts a connect check in
the dial context instead: DefaultSystemDialer runs it first in net.Dialer.Control before every connect attempt,
with the address Go resolved and is about to connect to (Go's own resolution, TcpRaceDial, a
sockopt.domainStrategy retarget, a sockopt.interface tunnel included), and on a UDP destination after
its one resolution. The check parses that exact address (netip.ParseAddrPort, zone stripped, IPv4-mapped
unmapped) and refuses the local host, a refused private address and anything it cannot parse, so the socket never
sends a SYN there; a refusal ends the dial without the retries, so an open and a closed private port look the same.
On every other path (proxySettings, dialerProxy, another TCP transport, an alternative system dialer, a dialer
freedom cannot classify) freedom resolves a name once with the system resolver, refuses when any answer is the
local host or a refused private address, refuses without dialing when the lookup fails or is empty, and dials only
the checked addresses, so nothing resolves the name again. app/proxyman/outbound's UDP-over-TCP server (the sing
uot magic names) writes its datagrams through the same check, which judges each one as UDP and drops a refused one.
Freedom also checks the remote address of the dialed connection, except the unspecified address that dialerProxy
and proxySettings connections report, and each UDP datagram after per-packet resolution, which is dropped. An XUDP
flow, whose context the mux server marks timeout-only so that it outlives the mux connection that opened it, is
detached with context.WithoutCancel instead of context.Background: it still ends only on inactivity, and it keeps
the session values, so its datagrams keep their allows. A refused connection
is logged at warning with the inbound's protocol and tag and "it is the private address" (or "local host",
"unparsable"). privateAllow is node-owned: the
PasarGuard node tags the routing rules that send explicit private addresses to a freedom outbound and lists them
there. app/router/private_target_match.go, app/proxyman/outbound/connect_check.go,
transport/internet/connect_check.go, infra/conf/freedom_private_target.go, proxy/freedom/private_target.go and
their tests are fork code; freedom.go, system_dialer.go and uot.go carry the hooks.

diff --git a/app/proxyman/outbound/connect_check.go b/app/proxyman/outbound/connect_check.go
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5e993f3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/app/proxyman/outbound/connect_check.go
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+package outbound
+
+import (
+	"github.com/sagernet/sing/common/uot"
+	"github.com/xtls/xray-core/common/net"
+	"github.com/xtls/xray-core/transport/internet"
+)
+
+func (h *Handler) AppliesConnectCheck(dest net.Destination) bool {
+	if h.senderSettings != nil && h.senderSettings.ProxySettings.HasTag() {
+		return false
+	}
+	if isUoTDestination(dest) {
+		return true
+	}
+	if h.streamSettings == nil {
+		return false
+	}
+	if sockopt := h.streamSettings.SocketSettings; sockopt != nil && len(sockopt.DialerProxy) > 0 {
+		return false
+	}
+	if dest.Network == net.Network_TCP && h.streamSettings.ProtocolName != "tcp" {
+		return false
+	}
+	return internet.SystemDialerAppliesConnectChecks()
+}
+
+func isUoTDestination(dest net.Destination) bool {
+	if dest.Address == nil || !dest.Address.Family().IsDomain() {
+		return false
+	}
+	switch dest.Address.Domain() {
+	case uot.MagicAddress, uot.LegacyMagicAddress:
+		return true
+	}
+	return false
+}
